# Changing the lint and format configuration

The configuration is strict by design and every choice is commented where it is made. Treat it
as source code with an audience: the comment must explain *why*, so the next reader does not
have to rediscover it.

## Do not weaken a check to make code pass

- A failing rule means the code is wrong. Fix the code.
- Never switch a rule to `'off'` in `vite.config.ts` to unblock yourself. The exception mechanism
  is a `// oxlint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>` comment at the line that needs it.
- `reportUnusedDisableDirectives` is `deny`: when a rule stops firing, the disable comment must
  be deleted with it.
- `denyWarnings` is on, so nothing is configured as a warning. A rule is an error or it is absent.
- A rule that genuinely does not fit this codebase is left **out of the list**, with a comment
  where it would have gone explaining why (see `no-implicit-coercion` in the package config).

## Where each thing lives

| Concern                                   | File                                                |
| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Formatting for the whole workspace        | root `vite.config.ts` → `fmt`                        |
| Library lint, build and test config       | `packages/valibot-to-openapi/vite.config.ts`         |
| Structural conventions a glob cannot express | `packages/valibot-to-openapi/lint/custom.js`      |
| Script-workspace lint                     | `test/vite.config.ts`, `example/vite.config.ts`      |
| Markdown structure / prose / spelling     | `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc`, `.textlintrc.yml`, `cspell.config.yaml` |
| Secrets, workflow lint                    | `.secretlintrc.json`, `scripts/actionlint.ts`        |

Formatting options live **only** in the root config; workspaces inherit them and declare nothing
but their `ignorePatterns`. `test/vite.config.ts` and `example/vite.config.ts` hold the same rule
list minus the library-internal rules — when you change one of them, change the other to match.

## The oxlint plugin

`packages/valibot-to-openapi/lint/custom.js` implements four repo conventions:
`custom/function-declaration`, `custom/predicate-is-name`, `custom/type-pascal-case`,
`custom/no-let`. It is an oxlint JS plugin using the alpha ESLint-compatible API — untyped
ESTree in, a default export out, which is why `lint/**` has its own override turning off the
`no-unsafe-*` and default-export rules.

When adding a rule there:

- Report a message that tells the author what to write instead, in the voice of the existing
  four — a sentence, not a rule name.
- Exempt `*.test.ts` if the rule describes the shape of `src` rather than a real hazard.
- Add it to the rules list in `vite.config.ts` under `custom/`, and to the header comment in
  `custom.js` that summarizes what the plugin enforces.
- Prove it: write code that should trip it, run `vp check`, and confirm the rule fires and the
  rest of the repository still passes.

## Architecture overrides

The `no-restricted-imports` overrides in `packages/valibot-to-openapi/vite.config.ts` encode the
one-way layer order. Widening one is a design change: move the shared code down a layer instead.
The `message` on each pattern must keep naming exactly which layers that directory may import.
